<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Binnya Dala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Binnya Dala was the last king of the Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom of the Mon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> from 1747. With French help he built up an army and in 1752 captured Ava, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toungoo-dynasty">Toungoo dynasty</a>. The Burman leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a> then reconquered the country and took Pegu in 1757. Binnya Dala was held captive and executed in 1774.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Binnya Dala, a Mon nobleman whose Burman given name was Aung Hla, is said to have come from Chiang Mai with a herd of elephants. In 1740 the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Nyaungyan dynasty</a>, also called the Restored Toungoo dynasty, and proclaimed a former Gwe Shan monk king with the title Smim Htaw Buddhaketi. The new king, popular but reluctant to govern, made Aung Hla master of the elephants, and he rose to be prime minister and the power behind the throne. When the king abdicated, the ministers proclaimed Binnya Dala king and made his younger brother heir with the title Upayaza.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Vowing to restore the empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>, Binnya Dala raised an army of sixty thousand men and a flotilla of war boats, bought firearms, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and ammunition from European traders, and took renegade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and native Portuguese into service. His forces took Ava in April 1752 and captured the last Nyaungyan king, Mahadhammaraza Dipadi. Believing the war won, he withdrew most of the army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, and the village chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Aung Zeya</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shwebo">Moksobo</a>, who took the name Alaungpaya and founded the Konbaung dynasty, drove the Hanthawaddy forces out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> by December 1753.</p><p>A second invasion in March 1754 went well at first but was driven back with heavy losses. The Hanthawaddy leadership then put the captive Mahadhammaraza Dipadi and other prisoners to death and required <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamar-people">Burmans</a> in the south to wear an earring stamped with the heir's seal and cut their hair in Mon fashion, policies that pushed many into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaungpaya">Alaungpaya</a>'s camp. Alaungpaya took the Irrawaddy delta in May 1755 and then stormed the port of Syriam, which the Frenchman Sieur de Bruno had helped to defend.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Konbaung armies besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> from September 1756 until famine brought the city to misery. Binnya Dala sent his only unmarried daughter as a peace offering, but fighting resumed, and in May 1757 the besiegers stormed a gate. The city was plundered, many leading men were killed and thousands of men, women and children were sold as slaves. Binnya Dala was imprisoned and spent over 17 years under house arrest. After a Mon rebellion in 1773 tried to restore him, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hsinbyushin">Hsinbyushin</a> had him charged with inciting it, and he was publicly executed in December 1774.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Binnya Dala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After years of inconclusive war, the southern army of King <b>Binnya Dala</b> advanced north and captured Ava in 1752.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Smim Htaw was replaced in 1747 by <b>Binnya Dala</b>, who with French assistance from Pondicherry built up an army and in 1752 captured Ava, deporting the last Toungoo king.</blockquote><a href="/state/restored-hanthawaddy-kingdom">Restored Hanthawaddy Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chiang Mai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.79,98.98&amp;year=1764" aria-label="Chiang Mai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Binnya Dala"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5724 5924h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5724 5924h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.79,98.98&amp;year=1764">What was here in 1764? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
